    [
     1,
     "Hi John,<p>It really helps if you provide your data in a way that we can easily<br>use - it saves a lot of time mucking around to get the data into R.  I<br>also suspect that you want 100&#39;s in the data frame and not 1&#39;s, and<br>the new row makes more sense at the top of the existing data.   I&#39;ve<br>given one approach to solve your problem with the plyr package<br>(<a href=\"http://had.co.nz/plyr\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://had.co.nz/plyr&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792359366382000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1mUW8aDX7WKa44pyRz59qj\">http://had.co.nz/plyr</a>).<p>df &lt;- read.table(textConnection(&quot;</p></p>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<p><p><br>           ID dose             Avep1              Avep2<br>Avep3             Avep4<br></p></p>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<p><p>1  50676-A-1    10  76.6030534351145   70.9993322946806<br>72.3357981731187  83.0880635365569<br>2  50676-A-1    50  12.0801526717557    7.6118406409971<br>7.69899956502827  17.7762205092268<br>3  50676-A-1    75  1.35496183206107   1.13788114845315<br>2.34884732492388  4.97547302032236<br>4  50676-A-1   100 0.598759541984733  0.567549521477854<br>0.622553284036538  2.03807521607101<br>5  50676-A-1   120  0.65601145038168 0.0584242154462498<br>1.40550239234450 0.908082223779492<br>6  50676-A-1   140 0.846851145038168  0.303249499221010<br>0.266420182688125  1.86580238262088<br>7  50676-A-1   160 0.372137404580153   1.00990429557089<br>0.492061765985211  2.42641906096706<br>8  50676-A-1   200 0.319656488549619  0.614845314934342<br>0.58721183123097  1.07159542163046<br>11 50676-A-2    10  84.1897233201581   85.3687739463602<br>87.1918801353311  90.1436464088398<br>12 50676-A-2    50  18.4106511337633   17.0019157088123<br>16.2155630739488  24.6408839779006<br>13 50676-A-2    75  1.91387559808613   1.96360153256705<br>1.15998066698888  2.43093922651934<br>14 50676-A-2   100 0.434262533804868   1.38888888888889<br>0.700821652972451  1.13812154696133<br>15 50676-A-2   120 0.309444560016643   0.58668582375479<br>0.640405993233447  1.30386740331492<br>16 50676-A-2   140 0.306844185562721  0.562739463601533<br>0.447075882068633 0.674033149171271<br>17 50676-A-2   160 0.260037445392137  0.311302681992337<br>0.809569840502659 0.773480662983426<br>18 50676-A-2   200 0.473268150613689  0.454980842911878<br>0.749154180763654 0.895027624309393<br>21 50676-A-3    10  88.8305225324855   74.5594713656388<br>83.3893050891243  81.4887848163668<br>22 50676-A-3    50  14.5700857063865   16.5198237885463<br>20.5114957375355  22.8493961054967<br>23 50676-A-3    75 0.940005529444292   2.17511013215859<br>2.9708085765952  1.72541286665024<br>24 50676-A-3   100 0.708460049764999   0.85352422907489<br>0.800826659777836 0.665516391422234<br>25 50676-A-3   120  0.63588609344761   0.49559471365639<br>0.561870317747353 0.391298989401036<br>26 50676-A-3   140 0.311031241360244  0.385462555066082<br>0.213123223973134 0.289622874044861<br>27 50676-A-3   160 0.228089576997513  0.426762114537447<br>0.180831826401447 0.511461671185606<br>28 50676-A-3   200 0.124412496544098  0.357929515418503<br>0.0129165590286748 0.406704461424698&quot;), header=T, row.names = 1)<br>closeAllConnections()<p># With this sort of problem it&#39;s always easiest to start by working on a<br># single subset of the data<br>one &lt;- subset(df, ID == &quot;50676-A-1&quot;)<p># Create a row for zero dose<br>zerodose &lt;- data.frame(<br>  ID = one$ID[1], dose = 0,<br>  Avep1 = 100, Avep2 = 100, Avep3 = 100, Avep4 = 100)<br># Combine with original data<br>rbind(zerodose, one)<p># Now you&#39;ve solved the problem for a single subset<br># So the next step is to turn it into a function:<p>add_zero_dose &lt;- function(one) {<br>  zerodose &lt;- data.frame(<br>    ID = one$ID[1], dose = 0,<br>    Avep1 = 100, Avep2 = 100, Avep3 = 100, Avep4 = 100)<br>  # Combine with original data<br>  rbind(zerodose, one)<br>}<p># and test it:<br>add_zero_dose(one)<p># You can then apply that function to every subset with<br># ddply from the plyr package<br>ddply(df, .(ID), add_zero_dose)<p># Check it looks ok with some quick exploratory graphics<br>library(ggplot2)<br>dfm &lt;- melt(df, id = c(&quot;dose&quot;,&quot;ID&quot;))<br>qplot(dose, value, data = dfm, colour = variable, facets = ~ ID, geom = &quot;line&quot;)<br>qplot(dose, value, data = dfm, colour = ID, geom = &quot;line&quot;, stat =<br>&quot;summary&quot;, fun.y = mean)<p><br>Hadley<p><br>-- <br><a href=\"http://had.co.nz/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://had.co.nz/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792359366383000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3wlq9QYIXyh38zj3IVOz-v\">http://had.co.nz/</a><br></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p>"
